you can see that in this case, we have once eight&nbsp;
ones, all in a line. Now on the left hand side&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.760 --> 00:05:02.000
we have what's called the most significant bit&nbsp;
that means that if one In this leftmost place,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:08.880
it's going to have a value of 128. If you have&nbsp;
a one, on the right hand side, that's the least&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:15.600
significant bit, and its value is only one. If&nbsp;
you have a zero anywhere, it just means zero. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:22.480
for example, in this address, the third octet is&nbsp;
a zero. So that means all of the eight bits are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.480 --> 00:05:29.760
zeros. The one means that all seven bits here are&nbsp;
zeros. And we have a one on the right hand side,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:35.360
and that equals this least significant&nbsp;
bit here. On the very left, we have 192.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.160 --> 00:05:43.280
Well, 192 is 128 plus 64. So we have a one&nbsp;
and a one, and the remaining values are zeros.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:50.880
And then for 168, we have a one, so that's 128.&nbsp;
Then we have a one in the third place here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.880 --> 00:05:59.200
which is 32. And then we have a one in the fifth&nbsp;
place here, which is eight. So we have 128 plus 32&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.200 --> 00:06:05.600
plus eight equals 168. The reason it's&nbsp;
important to understand this binary is&nbsp;&nbsp;

