increase or decrease the size of an existing&nbsp;
cider block. So you must get it right from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:05.120 --> 00:21:11.680
the beginning, the first four and last IP address&nbsp;
are not available for use. So you got to account&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:11.680 --> 00:21:16.880
for that when you set the size of your subnets.&nbsp;
Because you want to make sure you have enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:16.880 --> 00:21:24.720
addresses for your instances that you deploy. AWS&nbsp;
recommend that you use cider blocks from the RFC&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:24.720 --> 00:21:31.760
1918 ranges. Remember, these are the private IP&nbsp;
ranges. And that's these addresses. And of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:31.760 --> 00:21:37.600
these give you plenty of scope. So that shouldn't&nbsp;
be a problem. Now let's have a look at an example.&nbsp;&nbsp;

