00:21:55.280 --> 00:22:01.680
we want to create our subnets. So what we need to&nbsp;
do there is we're going to take some of the bits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:01.680 --> 00:22:08.320
from the host portion here and assign them to the&nbsp;
network portion. So maybe we're going to have 10&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:08.320 --> 00:22:18.320
010 slash 24. And that means we borrowed all eight&nbsp;
bits from that third octet for the network ID.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:18.320 --> 00:22:24.720
So your VPC subnets, will have a longer subnet&nbsp;
mask than the cider block that they come from.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:24.720 --> 00:22:29.680
Now, in this case, it's a 24 bit subnet&nbsp;
mask. Now what's the next network,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:29.680 --> 00:22:37.760
the next network is 10 020 slash 24. And then&nbsp;
the next one is 10, zero free, zero slash 24.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:38.640 --> 00:22:46.240
So each of these subnets is going to have&nbsp;
up to 254 potential hosts. But of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:46.240 --> 00:22:50.880
you have to take away a few from that number.&nbsp;
So take away five from that number. And that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:50.880 --> 00:22:56.560
how many addresses you have remaining for your&nbsp;
EC two instances. So as long as you're happy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:56.560 --> 00:23:00.080
with that number of instances per network,&nbsp;
which is quite a big number still,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:00.640 --> 00:23:06.240
then this is a good and very simple range to&nbsp;
use. Now let's go through a few additional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:06.240 --> 00:23:12.160
considerations. You got to ensure you have enough&nbsp;
networks and hosts, you really don't want to mess&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:12.160 --> 00:23:17.920
this up. You only get to set the cider block once&nbsp;
and then you're stuck with it. So make sure you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:17.920 --> 00:23:23.600
plan correctly. Bigger cider blocks are typically&nbsp;
better gives you more flexibility down the road.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:24.480 --> 00:23:30.240
Smaller subnets are okay for most use cases&nbsp;
you don't need 1000s of instances per subnet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:31.280 --> 00:23:37.840
Consider deploying application tiers per subnet.&nbsp;
So if you have a free tier application, you might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:37.840 --> 00:23:42.960
have different subnets for each tier. And you&nbsp;
might have it as highly available across multiple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:42.960 --> 00:23:48.960
availability zones as well with a subnet in each&nbsp;
AZ. So that's a good way of getting that high&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:48.960 --> 00:23:55.360
availability and resilience into your workload.&nbsp;
Now VPC peering, which we're going to cover a bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:55.360 --> 00:24:01.760
later, is where you connect your VP C's so that&nbsp;
you can route directly between them using private&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:01.760 --> 00:24:07.200
IP addresses, so you don't go out to the public&nbsp;
Internet. Now it's really essential that you don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:07.200 --> 00:24:13.360
have overlapping cider blocks. So in other words,&nbsp;
you can't have the same cider block in two v PCs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:13.360 --> 00:24:19.040
and connect them with VPC peering. So even if&nbsp;
you're not doing peering now, just remember this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:19.040 --> 00:24:24.160
because you might need to do it in the future. You&nbsp;
want to make sure your cider blocks don't overlap.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:24.160 --> 00:24:30.800
And this is across all v PCs and all regions and&nbsp;
all accounts so make sure you get it correct. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:30.800 --> 00:24:37.120
in general, just avoid overlapping cider blocks as&nbsp;
much as possible. Make sure you consider this when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:37.120 --> 00:24:43.200
you're designing your IP address ranges. So let's&nbsp;
go back to an example. Let's say we have 10 000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:44.560 --> 00:24:51.360
slash 16. So that's our cider block. And we create&nbsp;
a series of subnets. Now each of these subnets&nbsp;&nbsp;

