00:45:52.160 --> 00:45:57.360
So let's see how we can control traffic.&nbsp;
Firstly, we can apply something called a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:57.360 --> 00:46:04.080
network access control list or network ACL. Now&nbsp;
these get applied at the subnet level. So you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:04.080 --> 00:46:12.320
see that they're sitting here at the edge of the&nbsp;
subnet. Now, all traffic that goes into and out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:12.320 --> 00:46:20.320
a subnet will go through a network ACL. So you're&nbsp;
essentially filtering traffic as it enters or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:20.320 --> 00:46:26.640
exits the subnet. So ingress and egress traffic,&nbsp;
a security group is different. A security group&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:26.640 --> 00:46:32.400
actually applies at the instance level of an EC&nbsp;
two instance, actually at the network interface&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:32.400 --> 00:46:39.920
level of the EC two instance. So that means we&nbsp;
can have a security group that is applied to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:39.920 --> 00:46:46.080
EC two instances in different subnets. So this&nbsp;
is the same security group Security Group A,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:46.080 --> 00:46:49.680
and it's been applied to instances&nbsp;
in several different subnets.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:50.400 --> 00:46:56.640
So remember that this is the instance level, that&nbsp;
means it's going to filter traffic going between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:56.640 --> 00:47:02.720
instances in the same subnet, or across different&nbsp;
subnets, we could then have security group B,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:02.720 --> 00:47:08.720
that's attached to different instances. So&nbsp;
security groups can be applied to instances in any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:08.720 --> 00:47:14.800
subnet. Now, what happens here with these security&nbsp;
groups, is the same basic thing as a network ACL.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:15.360 --> 00:47:19.840
They're looking for traffic that's going&nbsp;
in and out of the security group. So if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:19.840 --> 00:47:24.880
you send traffic from an instance, in security&nbsp;
group A, to an instance, in security group B,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:25.440 --> 00:47:30.240
it's going to check that there's a rule to allow&nbsp;
the outbound traffic, and then it's going to check&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:30.240 --> 00:47:35.760
here, the Security Group B is going to check Am I&nbsp;
going to allow the traffic from Security Group A,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:36.400 --> 00:47:41.600
and you can do that by IP address, or you can&nbsp;
actually do it by the ID of the security group&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:41.600 --> 00:47:47.040
itself, we'll look at all this in detail in the&nbsp;
hands on as well. Now I want to talk to you about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:47.040 --> 00:47:53.360
stateful versus state less firewalls, because&nbsp;
the security group is a stateful firewall. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:53.360 --> 00:47:57.680
a network ACL is a stateless firewall. And it's&nbsp;
really important to understand the difference.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:58.240 --> 00:48:04.000
So let's look at an example. We have a client&nbsp;
here. So this is a computer that wants to access&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:04.000 --> 00:48:09.520
a service on a web server, and we've got a&nbsp;
firewall in the middle. So what happens is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:09.520 --> 00:48:15.360
the client is going to connect to the server, the&nbsp;
server is a web server, it's listening on port 80.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:16.240 --> 00:48:21.307
The Source port is the port that's assigned&nbsp;
to the connection by the computer. And this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:21.307 --> 00:48:25.680
is what's called a high numbered port is&nbsp;
a port above 1024. And it's dynamically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:25.680 --> 00:48:30.720
assigned for every connection. So we have&nbsp;
a source port and a destination port.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:31.600 --> 00:48:38.560
And we have a source and destination IP as well.&nbsp;
Now the return traffic has a source port of Port&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:38.560 --> 00:48:45.600
80, because that's where the web service port&nbsp;
runs, and the destination port of 65188. And of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:45.600 --> 00:48:50.080
course the IPS are switched around as well.&nbsp;
So the destination IP will be the client,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:50.080 --> 00:48:55.440
the source IP will be the web server. Now what&nbsp;
do we need in the firewall rule table to allow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:55.440 --> 00:49:01.760
this to work? Well, what we need here is we&nbsp;
need a rule for HTTP that allows the source&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:01.760 --> 00:49:12.400
IP 10.1 dot 1.1 to talk to the destination IP&nbsp;
10 dot 2.1 dot 10. And it has to have the source&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:12.400 --> 00:49:17.520
port and the destination port specified. Now of&nbsp;
course, this is a dynamically assigned port. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:18.240 --> 00:49:23.440
we don't know what that is ahead of time. So what&nbsp;
you'd most likely have here is any so rather than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:23.440 --> 00:49:28.880
having the source port specified we'd have the&nbsp;
any specified so any source port is acceptable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:29.440 --> 00:49:34.283
And then rather than a specific source IP,&nbsp;
we might have any because it's an internet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:34.283 --> 00:49:38.800
based connection. So those are things that&nbsp;
we can play around with in our rule tables.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:38.800 --> 00:49:42.560
But the destination port will always be 80&nbsp;
because that's where the web server runs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:42.560 --> 00:49:47.520
So we want to make sure that we don't allow&nbsp;
connections to any ports on the web server.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:48.480 --> 00:49:52.720
Now that's the traffic coming inbound&nbsp;
to the web server. What about the return&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:52.720 --> 00:49:58.160
traffic going back? Well, in that case, things are&nbsp;
swapped around. So we've got the same protocol,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:58.160 --> 00:50:04.320
the source IP of the web server, the destination&nbsp;
IP of the client, and the source port of 80,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:04.320 --> 00:50:09.440
and the destination port of the dynamic port at&nbsp;
the clients. Now again, you'd probably have the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:09.440 --> 00:50:15.600
destination port, and the destination IP is any,&nbsp;
because you want to allow traffic coming from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:15.600 --> 00:50:20.640
any client on the internet if you're running a&nbsp;
public web service, but you still might need a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:20.640 --> 00:50:25.920
rule set for the outbound traffic. And this is&nbsp;
where the difference comes in between stateful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:25.920 --> 00:50:33.440
and stateless firewalls. So a stateful firewall&nbsp;
will allow the return traffic automatically. Now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:33.440 --> 00:50:38.160
this is what a security group is, what that&nbsp;
means is with a security group, you only need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:38.160 --> 00:50:43.680
this roll up the top here, you need a role that&nbsp;
says that you're going to allow the traffic from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:43.680 --> 00:50:48.880
whatever client on whatever port, but you're going&nbsp;
to allow inbound to Port 80 on your web server.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:49.840 --> 00:50:53.520
And then the return traffic is automatically&nbsp;
allowed. So you don't have to worry about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:53.520 --> 00:50:57.840
the return traffic is going to just go out&nbsp;
automatically, it's going to be allowed because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:57.840 --> 00:51:02.880
the firewall is clever enough to know that that's&nbsp;
part of the connection. And so it just allows that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:02.880 --> 00:51:09.680
return traffic. Now a state less viable checks&nbsp;
for an allow rule for both connections. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:09.680 --> 00:51:15.920
this is what a network ACL does. So we have&nbsp;
a network ACL, you need both the inbound rule&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:15.920 --> 00:51:20.960
and the outbound rule. So you need to make&nbsp;
sure that you have the inbound rule for traffic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:20.960 --> 00:51:25.840
coming in, and the outbound rule for traffic&nbsp;
going back. Even if it's the same connection,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:25.840 --> 00:51:30.960
it doesn't understand that that's part of the same&nbsp;
connection, it treats them separately. So really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:30.960 --> 00:51:36.800
important to understand those differences when you&nbsp;
configure your network ACLs. Now let's have a look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:36.800 --> 00:51:42.400
at a security group. This is what a security group&nbsp;
rule set looks like. So here we have separate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:42.400 --> 00:51:47.920
rules defined for inbound and outbound traffic,&nbsp;
what we're looking at here is the inbound traffic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

