but just remember that they will be processed&nbsp;
in order. Now, the important facts about that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:33.440 --> 00:55:40.000
means that once you actually reach a rule that&nbsp;
either allows or denies traffic, the processing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:40.000 --> 00:55:46.880
stops there. So if we have an allow rule at 100,&nbsp;
that allows the traffic, then even if you had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:46.880 --> 00:55:52.400
a deny rule, later on in the rule set for that&nbsp;
specific traffic, it's not going to get there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:52.400 --> 00:55:57.440
it's going to allow it because it reaches DLL&nbsp;
and processing finishes. So you've got to be very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:57.440 --> 00:56:02.400
careful with how you set up your rules the order&nbsp;
that you create them in, and just remember that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:02.400 --> 00:56:07.120
they will be processed in order, and then when&nbsp;
an allow or deny is reached, then that's the end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:07.120 --> 00:56:11.360
of the processing, it will either allow or deny&nbsp;
the connection. So that's it for the theory. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

