01:41:52.720 --> 01:41:59.520
AWS VPN cloud hub is not actually a service that&nbsp;
you can go and find in the AWS console. What this&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:59.520 --> 01:42:06.784
really is, is a pattern and architectural pattern&nbsp;
that you can use when you utilize the AWS site to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:06.784 --> 01:42:12.880
site VPN technology. So let's have a look at the&nbsp;
architecture in which you can implement cloud hub.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:13.600 --> 01:42:20.240
So we've got a VPC. And we've implemented a&nbsp;
virtual private gateway a VG W. And that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:20.240 --> 01:42:26.800
obviously deployed on the AWS side. We then&nbsp;
have multiple customer offices, or these could&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:26.800 --> 01:42:32.800
be customer data centers. But they're different&nbsp;
on premises environments that we want to connect&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:32.800 --> 01:42:39.760
using a virtual private network to AWS. Now, the&nbsp;
way that we connect them in a cloud hub topology,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:39.760 --> 01:42:45.920
is using a hub and spoke model. So in&nbsp;
other words, AWS is the hub, the VPC.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:46.480 --> 01:42:52.720
And then the spokes go out to each of these&nbsp;
offices. So what we do is we deploy a customer&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:52.720 --> 01:43:00.000
gateway into each of these offices. Each of&nbsp;
these customer gateways has a unique BGP ASN,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:00.000 --> 01:43:06.960
that's a Border Gateway Protocol autonomous system&nbsp;
number. So BGP is a protocol that you can use for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:06.960 --> 01:43:14.960
advertising routes to different parts of your&nbsp;
network. And each environment has its own ASN.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:14.960 --> 01:43:21.120
And that corresponds with the routes that would be&nbsp;
advertised into the network, so the IP prefixes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:21.680 --> 01:43:29.520
so you must have a unique BGP ASN for each&nbsp;
office. And then we can establish a VPN connection&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:29.520 --> 01:43:35.120
between each of those customer gateways and&nbsp;
the same Virtual Gateway on the AWS side.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:35.120 --> 01:43:41.920
Now network traffic can originate from an office&nbsp;
and go to the VPC or the VPC back to an office.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:42.480 --> 01:43:48.720
But also, it can go between the customer offices&nbsp;
or the customer on premises environments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:48.720 --> 01:43:56.960
whether their offices or data centers. So the&nbsp;
customer office here can communicate via the VG W.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:56.960 --> 01:44:04.880
So this custom office here. So we're using the AWS&nbsp;
site to site VPN to establish a routing topology,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:04.880 --> 01:44:12.560
which connects multiple customer offices, as well&nbsp;
as the VPC itself. Now, this is using IPsec VPN,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:12.560 --> 01:44:18.320
the same VPN, as we just looked at in previous&nbsp;
lessons. So that's it. That's VPN cloud hub. As&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:18.320 --> 01:44:23.200
I mentioned, if you go around looking in the&nbsp;
AWS console, you won't find cloud hub. But if&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:23.200 --> 01:44:28.080
you actually do a search online, you'll find some&nbsp;
articles about this architecture, which can give&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:28.080 --> 01:44:33.680
you a bit more insight as to how it's set up. But&nbsp;
basically, we're just connecting multiple customer&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:33.680 --> 01:44:39.680
gateways to a single virtual private gateway.&nbsp;
And then it's up to you to configure your BGP&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:39.680 --> 01:44:44.880
protocol accordingly to advertise the&nbsp;
routes that you want to advertise into&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:44.880 --> 01:44:49.840
the network. So that's it for a quick overview&nbsp;
of cloud hub. I'll see you in the next lesson.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:52.320 --> 01:44:58.880
We've already talked about virtual private network&nbsp;
connections VPN. Now VPN is typically run over the&nbsp;&nbsp;

