01:59:27.760 --> 01:59:34.960
running short of ipv4 addresses. So we're going&nbsp;
to have a look at how you can use ipv6 in a V PC.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:36.000 --> 01:59:42.880
So let's start by looking at the structure of&nbsp;
the IP v4 address. So remember, it's made up of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:42.880 --> 01:59:50.160
four numbers separated by dots. And those numbers&nbsp;
come from an octet. So that's eight binary bits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:50.160 --> 01:59:57.360
which are either one or zero. So an ipv4 address&nbsp;
is 32 bits long. Now let's have a look at how many&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:57.360 --> 02:00:02.480
addresses you get for that you get 4.3 billion&nbsp;
addresses, which sounds like a big number.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:02.480 --> 02:00:07.280
And it's a big number of its dollars in your bank&nbsp;
account. But when it's addresses that needs to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:07.280 --> 02:00:12.000
assigned to all sorts of different devices all&nbsp;
over the world, in a world full of 7 billion&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:12.000 --> 02:00:17.680
people, it's just not anywhere near enough. And&nbsp;
it might have sounded like more than enough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:17.680 --> 02:00:23.680
quite a few years ago, but it was became apparent,&nbsp;
even in the 90s, that it was not going to suffice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:23.680 --> 02:00:31.680
and it hasn't. So we are close to exhausting these&nbsp;
addresses. And Nat has to be used extensively. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:31.680 --> 02:00:38.400
if you're in a company, and your company has 1000s&nbsp;
of computers internally, then those computers will&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:38.400 --> 02:00:44.400
most likely be using ipv4 addresses. And those&nbsp;
will be private addresses. And when they access&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:44.400 --> 02:00:50.240
the internet, they go for a NAT device or some&nbsp;
sort. And that means that a public IP address&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:50.240 --> 02:00:57.680
is used to represent many, many PCs. So that's a&nbsp;
workaround that we have to put in place with ipv4.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:58.400 --> 02:01:04.960
With ipv6, we don't have to worry about that. So&nbsp;
let's look at the structure of an ipv6 address.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:05.600 --> 02:01:13.680
So this is the structure of ipv6. It also has&nbsp;
a network part and a node part. But it's 128&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:13.680 --> 02:01:21.600
bits long. So it's a much, much bigger number.&nbsp;
But also it uses hexadecimal. Whereas ipv4 uses&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:21.600 --> 02:01:27.840
dotted decimal. So that means there's a lot more&nbsp;
potential values that you can use in hexadecimal&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:27.840 --> 02:01:34.400
versus decimal. So that means the amount of&nbsp;
addresses is much, much bigger. And this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:34.400 --> 02:01:41.920
the number that's the number of addresses you can&nbsp;
get out of ipv6. And it's absolutely huge. So put&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:41.920 --> 02:01:46.880
it into some kind of perspective. And by the way,&nbsp;
if you look on Google for analogies for the size&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:46.880 --> 02:01:53.200
of the ipv6 address base, there's all sorts of&nbsp;
fantastic analogies, because it really is massive.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:53.200 --> 02:01:59.920
So one that I like is that you can get 100 ipv6&nbsp;
addresses for every atom on Earth. So that is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:59.920 --> 02:02:06.720
huge, huge number, which means we never ever have&nbsp;
to worry about running out of ipv6 addresses.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:06.720 --> 02:02:12.800
So let's have a look at how we need to set up our&nbsp;
VPC. So we've got a VPC with a couple of subnets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:12.800 --> 02:02:19.760
we need to have an ipv4 cider block. And then we&nbsp;
need the individual subnet ranges for our subnets.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:20.320 --> 02:02:27.840
Now, we also need an ipv6 cider block now, so&nbsp;
we've allocated one to this account. Now, AWS will&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:27.840 --> 02:02:37.520
assign a slash 56 ipv6 address range to the VPC,&nbsp;
then you can create your subnet ranges within that&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:37.520 --> 02:02:46.480
address space. And those become slash 64 subnets.&nbsp;
Now, a slash 64 subnet will allow 18 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:46.480 --> 02:02:52.560
trillion addresses. So again, it's an example of&nbsp;
why you really don't need to worry about how many&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:52.560 --> 02:02:58.000
addresses you're going to have for your computers,&nbsp;
because it's just huge. Now, you'll notice there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:58.000 --> 02:03:05.280
two numbers highlighted in red at the end of the&nbsp;
IP address. This is a hexadecimal pair, which has&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:05.280 --> 02:03:12.640
values from 00 to FF. And this designates the&nbsp;
individual subnet. So it has to be unique for&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:12.640 --> 02:03:20.400
each subnet. And it means that you're going to&nbsp;
have a possible 256 subnets, with ipv6 blocks in&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:20.400 --> 02:03:27.200
them. And each of those is a slash 64. So each of&nbsp;
those will support 18 million trillion addresses.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:27.200 --> 02:03:34.000
So lots of flexibility for your VPC. Now let's&nbsp;
look at the route table once we've enabled ipv6,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:34.000 --> 02:03:42.400
so we now have a local route for the ipv6 cider&nbsp;
block. And we need a route to the internet&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:42.400 --> 02:03:47.600
gateway for everything that's outside of that&nbsp;
range, just like we did for ipv4, we have the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:47.600 --> 02:03:55.360
colon colon slash zero for ipv6, pointing at the&nbsp;
Internet gateways ID. And that means that anything&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:55.360 --> 02:04:01.280
that's not an ipv6 address in this range will go&nbsp;
to the internet gateway. So we'll make sure that&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:01.280 --> 02:04:09.280
we have that specified in our route table when we&nbsp;
enable ipv6. Now all ipv6 addresses are publicly&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:09.280 --> 02:04:15.120
routable. That means there's no network address&nbsp;
translation. Now, if you want to, you can use&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:15.120 --> 02:04:22.480
something called an egress only internet gateway.&nbsp;
Now the purpose of this is when you want to allow&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:22.480 --> 02:04:30.480
outbound access only using ipv6. So it's kind of&nbsp;
like having a EC two instance in a private subnet&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:30.480 --> 02:04:36.880
with ipv4 and then adding a NAT gateway into a&nbsp;
public subnet. It means that that instance is&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:36.880 --> 02:04:41.600
protected from the internet, no one can access&nbsp;
it directly from the internet, but it does have&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:41.600 --> 02:04:47.920
internet access outbound. So that's what an egress&nbsp;
only internet gateway does. So that's it for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:47.920 --> 02:04:53.920
ferry on this topic, and in the next lesson,&nbsp;
we're going to go in and enable ipv6 for our VPC.&nbsp;&nbsp;

